112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (WHY DO WE SEEK VOLUNTEERS?)
/E (WHY DO WE SEEK VOLUNTEERS?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Why do we seek volunteers? To teach? To work? To serve? We have many needs and many tasks in our church, but if the sole reason we seek volunteers is to perform a task, then we are missing out on the primary reason we seek volunteers. The primary reason we seek volunteers is indeed to fill roles, but the deeper reason these roles exist is to give people a place of connection. Read that again slowly. Do you thoroughly understand the why?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (There are many tasks in the church that do not actually need as many hands on deck as one might think. For many years, Pastor Aaron handled multiple tasks on his own. Certainly, by his own profession, this was neither his desire nor an efficient way of pastoring, but for a season, help was limited. While people did step up, there were many Friday evenings when Pastor Aaron would come to the church to plow snow or arrive early for a last-minute touch-up. Perhaps trash had been missed, or something in the church broke. What a relief when Marie recruited a cleaning team and many faithful members stepped up and began improving the beauty of our facility. Others would come and set up flags, signs, and refreshments. Suddenly, the work that relied on Pastor was delegated to others, allowing him to focus on other areas of ministry. There's no denying that we have needs and must partner with people.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (However, there is another critical reason why ministry positions exist. When people come to church and are looking to give of their gifts and talents, we must be capable of receiving those gifts and deploying people into works of service and ministry. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ("Each of you should use whatever gift you have received to serve others, as faithful stewards of God's grace in its various forms."  - 1 Peter 4:10)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (If we do not prepare a space for individuals to use their gifts, then they will invest those gifts elsewhere. If we are tight-fisted and unwilling to delegate responsibilities, teach people, and train people, then we will lose people. Additionally, the purpose of ministry positions, in addition to helping people find their place in our community, is to provide an opportunity to establish deeper relationships. Genuine relationships are the pathway to discipleship. Leaders who understand that their role in the church is intended to provide them with the platform they need to begin doing life with others is radically important. You are a leader because you are potentially a disciple-maker.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (If you intend to continue in your role as a pastor or leader here at Garden City Church, then you must be prepared to make disciples for the Lord Jesus. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ("Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, and teaching them to obey everything I have commanded you. And surely I am with you always, to the very end of the age." - Matthew 28:19-20)
/Alt ()
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
